"""Author-written generic paragraphs for the C15-R A-stage (task-independent material).

Written 2026-10-04 before any model run. Diverse everyday / encyclopaedic topics; deliberately free of
logic, validity, verification and judgement vocabulary. Split into G_select / G_confirm / SMOKE by
tools/make_splits.py (seed 9300). Each paragraph is long enough to yield >= 64 tokens.
"""

PARAGRAPHS = [
    # geography and nature
    "The river begins as a thin stream high in the mountains, fed by melting snow each spring. As it travels toward the lowlands it collects water from dozens of smaller creeks, widening into a slow brown channel that winds past farms and villages. Near the coast it splits into many branches, forming a broad delta where reeds grow taller than a person and herons wade through the shallow pools.",
    "Deserts cover a large part of the land surface, yet they are far from empty. Cacti store water in thick stems, small rodents spend the hottest hours in cool burrows, and many plants wait years for a single heavy rain. When that rain finally arrives, the ground can burst into colour within days, as seeds that have rested in the sand sprout, flower and scatter new seeds before the soil dries again.",
    "The island sits about forty kilometres off the coast and can be reached by a ferry that leaves twice a day. Its northern side is rocky and windswept, with cliffs where seabirds nest in large colonies. The southern side is gentler, with sandy coves, a small fishing harbour and a single road that runs between olive groves and whitewashed houses toward the old lighthouse on the far point.",
    "In the far north, winter nights last for many weeks and the sun barely rises above the horizon. People who live there adapt their routines, keeping homes warm with wood stoves and spending long evenings with family and neighbours. When spring returns the light comes back quickly, and by midsummer the sun circles the sky without setting, so children play outside until what would elsewhere be the middle of the night.",
    "A tropical rainforest holds an astonishing variety of life in layers. The tallest trees push their crowns above the canopy to catch full sunlight, while vines and climbing plants race upward along their trunks. Below, the air is humid and dim, and the forest floor is covered in fallen leaves that decay quickly. Frogs, insects, birds and monkeys each occupy their own level of this green tower.",
    "Volcanoes form where molten rock from deep inside the planet finds a path to the surface. Some erupt explosively, throwing ash high into the atmosphere, while others release slow rivers of lava that creep across the landscape. Over thousands of years, repeated eruptions can build mountains, create new islands in the ocean and leave behind soil so rich that farmers return to the slopes despite the danger.",
    "The lake is frozen solid for three months each year, and during that time the townspeople drive their cars across it to reach the far shore. Fishermen drill holes through the ice and sit inside small wooden huts, waiting patiently with short rods and thermos flasks of hot tea. When the thaw arrives in April, the ice cracks with loud booming sounds that can be heard across the valley.",
    "Coral reefs grow in warm, shallow seas where sunlight reaches the sea floor. Each reef is built over centuries by tiny animals that produce hard skeletons, layer upon layer. The structure provides shelter for fish of every colour, as well as turtles, eels and countless smaller creatures. Divers who visit often describe the experience as swimming through an underwater city full of movement and noise.",
    "Mountain weather can change in minutes. A clear morning may turn cloudy by noon, and by mid afternoon a storm can roll over the ridge with hail and strong gusts of wind. Experienced hikers start early, carry extra layers of clothing and keep an eye on the sky, turning back when dark clouds gather. Many of them say that the mountains teach patience more than any other place.",
    "Grasslands stretch across the middle of several continents, with rolling plains that seem to reach the horizon in every direction. Herds of grazing animals move across them with the seasons, following the fresh growth that appears after the rains. Fires sweep through from time to time, clearing old stems and returning nutrients to the soil, so that the grass returns greener the following year.",
    "The waterfall drops nearly a hundred metres from a ledge of hard rock into a deep green pool. A fine mist rises from the bottom and drifts across the path, so visitors who walk close to the edge come back with damp hair and clothes. On sunny afternoons a rainbow often hangs in the spray, and photographers wait for hours to capture it at the perfect moment.",
    "Caves are formed slowly as water seeps through cracks in limestone and dissolves the rock. Over long periods the cracks widen into passages and chambers, some large enough to hold a cathedral. Drips of mineral water create pointed formations that hang from the ceiling or rise from the floor. Bats sleep in the darkest corners during the day and fly out at dusk to hunt insects.",
    "Along the coast the tide rises and falls twice each day, uncovering wide stretches of wet sand and rock pools. Children search the pools for crabs, starfish and small fish trapped until the water returns. Seaweed clings to the rocks, and gulls patrol the shoreline looking for anything edible. Locals know the timing of the tides well, because the sea can return faster than a person can walk.",
    "The valley was shaped by a glacier that retreated thousands of years ago, leaving behind steep walls, scattered boulders and a long narrow lake. Today the slopes are covered with pine forest, and a small road follows the lake shore to a village at the far end. In autumn the larch trees turn bright yellow, and the reflection of the hills in the still water draws painters from far away.",
    "Wetlands act like giant sponges, soaking up heavy rains and releasing water slowly during dry periods. They filter sediment and provide breeding grounds for frogs, insects and birds. Many migrating ducks and geese stop at marshes to rest and feed on long journeys between their summer and winter homes. Protecting these areas has become a priority for many regions that have lost much of their original marshland.",
    "On the high plateau the air is thin and the sunlight feels unusually strong. Shepherds lead their flocks across the open ground, sleeping in stone shelters during the summer months. The nights are cold even in July, and the sky is so clear that the band of the galaxy stretches visibly from one horizon to the other. Few roads cross the region, and travel is still measured in days rather than hours.",
    "Autumn arrives in the forest with a gradual change of colours. Maple leaves turn red and orange, birches become golden, and oaks hold their brown leaves long after the others have fallen. Squirrels gather nuts and bury them in hidden places, while deer grow thicker coats for the coming cold. On calm mornings fog settles in the hollows, and the smell of damp earth fills the air.",
    "The bay is sheltered from the open ocean by a long sandy spit that curves around it like an arm. Inside, the water is calm and shallow, ideal for small sailing boats and swimmers. Fishing boats leave before dawn and return in the late morning with their catch, which is sold directly on the quay. In summer the beach fills with families, umbrellas and the sound of ice cream vans.",
    "Earthquakes happen when large plates of rock that make up the outer shell of the planet suddenly slip past one another. Most are too small to notice, but strong ones can shake buildings and trigger landslides. In regions where they are common, engineers design flexible structures, schools hold regular drills and families keep emergency supplies of water, food and torches in an easily reached cupboard.",
    "The savanna at dawn is quiet except for birdsong and the distant call of a lion. As the sun rises, herds of zebra and antelope move toward the waterhole, watched from a distance by patient predators. By midday the heat becomes intense, and most animals rest in the shade of scattered acacia trees. Activity returns in the late afternoon, when the light softens and the air cools a little.",
    # history
    "The old castle was built on a hill above the river more than eight hundred years ago. Its thick stone walls protected the local lord and his household during many sieges. Over the centuries it was extended with new towers, a great hall and a chapel, then partly abandoned when warfare changed. Today it is a museum, and visitors climb the narrow spiral stairs to enjoy the view from the battlements.",
    "Ancient traders crossed deserts and mountains carrying silk, spices, paper and precious metals between distant empires. The journeys took months, and goods often passed through many hands before reaching their final buyers. Along the way, travellers exchanged not only merchandise but also languages, religions, recipes and inventions. Cities that grew up at important crossroads became wealthy and famous for their markets and caravan inns.",
    "The printing press transformed the spread of ideas in Europe during the fifteenth century. Before it, books were copied slowly by hand, and only wealthy institutions could afford large libraries. With movable type, a single workshop could produce hundreds of copies in the time a scribe needed for one. Pamphlets, maps and textbooks soon circulated widely, and reading became a skill that many more people wanted to learn.",
    "In the early industrial towns, large mills drew workers from the countryside with the promise of steady wages. Families crowded into narrow streets of brick houses close to the factories, and the air was thick with smoke from countless chimneys. Over time, reformers campaigned for shorter working hours, safer machines and schools for children, and many of their ideas eventually became part of ordinary life.",
    "The ancient city was laid out in a grid of straight streets, with a central square surrounded by temples, a market and public baths. Water arrived through a long aqueduct from springs in the hills, and a system of drains carried waste away. Archaeologists working there have found mosaics, coins, cooking pots and graffiti scratched into walls, which reveal a great deal about everyday life two thousand years ago.",
    "Sailors in the age of exploration navigated using the stars, simple instruments and a great deal of courage. Voyages across unknown oceans could last many months, and crews suffered from storms, hunger and disease. Maps of the time were often inaccurate, with large blank areas and imaginary islands. Each successful return added coastlines, currents and harbours to the charts used by those who followed.",
    "The great wall was not built all at once but in many stages over different dynasties. Workers used earth, stone, brick and wood, depending on what was available nearby. Watchtowers placed along its length allowed soldiers to send signals with smoke by day and fire by night. Although its military value changed over time, it remains one of the most impressive construction projects in history.",
    "Medieval monasteries were centres of learning as well as worship. Monks copied manuscripts, kept records, tended gardens of medicinal herbs and brewed beer. Travellers could often find shelter for a night in exchange for a small donation. Some monasteries grew very wealthy from the lands they owned, and their libraries preserved many ancient texts that might otherwise have been lost.",
    "The first railways appeared in the early nineteenth century and quickly changed the way people lived. Journeys that once took days by horse could now be completed in hours. Towns along the new lines prospered, while those that were bypassed often declined. Timetables even led to the standardisation of clocks across whole countries, since trains could not run safely if every town kept its own local time.",
    "Explorers who first reached the polar regions faced extreme cold, long periods of darkness and the constant danger of shifting ice. They relied on dogs, sledges and carefully planned food depots. Several expeditions ended in tragedy, while others returned with valuable scientific observations about weather, magnetism and wildlife. Their diaries, written by candlelight in freezing tents, are still read with fascination today.",
    "The marketplace in the old town has been held on the same square every Saturday for over five hundred years. Farmers bring vegetables, cheese, eggs and flowers, while craftsmen sell baskets, pottery and woollen goods. In the past, disputes about weights and prices were settled by a market official who kept standard measures in a small stone building that still stands at the corner of the square.",
    "During the long war, many families sent their children to the countryside for safety. The children lived with strangers on farms, attended village schools and wrote letters home every week. Some stayed for years and formed lifelong friendships with their hosts. When peace finally came, the railway stations were crowded with parents waiting anxiously for the trains that brought their sons and daughters back.",
    "Lighthouses were once tended by keepers who lived beside them in all seasons. Every evening they climbed the stairs to light the lamp, trimmed the wicks during the night and polished the great lenses in the morning. In storms they might stay awake for days. Today most lights work automatically, but some old keepers' cottages have been turned into small museums or holiday homes.",
    "The ancient library was said to hold hundreds of thousands of scrolls collected from across the known world. Scholars came to study astronomy, medicine, geography and poetry, and some of them made discoveries that are still remembered. Ships arriving in the harbour were sometimes required to lend their books so that copies could be made. Its eventual loss became a symbol of how fragile knowledge can be.",
    "Before refrigerators, people stored food in cellars, salted meat and fish, pickled vegetables and dried fruit in the sun. In some towns, blocks of ice were cut from frozen lakes during winter and kept in insulated ice houses under layers of straw. Ice was then delivered to homes by cart during the warm months. The arrival of electric cooling changed kitchens and shopping habits within a single generation.",
    # science
    "Light from the sun takes about eight minutes to reach the earth. Although it looks white, it contains a mixture of colours that can be separated by a prism or by raindrops to form a rainbow. Plants capture part of this light with the green pigment in their leaves and use its energy to turn water and carbon dioxide into sugars, releasing oxygen into the air as they do.",
    "The human heart beats around one hundred thousand times every day, pumping blood through a network of vessels that would stretch many thousands of kilometres if laid end to end. Blood carries oxygen from the lungs to every cell and returns carbon dioxide to be breathed out. Regular exercise strengthens the heart muscle, much as lifting weights strengthens the muscles of the arms and legs.",
    "Sound travels as waves of pressure through air, water or solid materials. It moves faster in water than in air and faster still through steel. Bats and dolphins use echoes to find their way and locate prey, sending out clicks and listening to how they bounce back. Musical instruments produce different notes by changing the length or tension of strings or the size of vibrating air columns.",
    "Magnets attract iron because of the way tiny regions inside the metal line up. The earth itself behaves like a giant magnet, which is why a compass needle points roughly north. Some animals, including certain birds and turtles, appear to sense this magnetic field and use it to find their way during long migrations. Electric motors and generators also rely on the interaction between magnetism and electric currents.",
    "Bacteria are single cells so small that thousands could fit across the width of a human hair. Most are harmless, and many are useful: they help digest food in the gut, break down dead plants and animals, and are used to make yoghurt and cheese. A few cause illness, which is why washing hands and cooking food thoroughly remain simple but powerful habits for staying healthy.",
    "Stars are enormous balls of hot gas that produce energy by fusing light elements into heavier ones in their cores. Their colour shows their temperature, with blue stars being the hottest and red stars cooler. Over millions or billions of years they change, swelling or collapsing as their fuel runs low. The heavy elements in our own bodies were forged in stars that lived and died long before the sun formed.",
    "Water is unusual because it expands when it freezes, which is why ice floats on lakes instead of sinking. This floating layer insulates the water below, allowing fish to survive the winter. Water also absorbs a great deal of heat before its temperature rises, so oceans warm and cool slowly and help keep coastal climates mild. Without these properties, life on earth would be very different.",
    "The moon has no air and no liquid water, so its surface is covered with craters that have remained almost unchanged for billions of years. It always shows the same face to the earth because it rotates once for every orbit. Its gravity pulls on the oceans, creating tides, and its light has guided travellers and farmers for thousands of years before electric lighting was invented.",
    "Vaccines train the immune system to recognise particular germs without causing serious illness. When the body later meets the real germ, it can respond quickly and strongly. The idea goes back more than two centuries to observations that milkmaids who had caught a mild disease from cows seemed protected against a much more dangerous one. Since then, vaccination has become one of the most important tools of public health.",
    "Plants move water from their roots to their highest leaves without any pump. Water evaporates from tiny pores in the leaves, and this pulls a continuous column of water upward through narrow tubes inside the stem. Tall trees can lift hundreds of litres a day in this way. On hot afternoons some plants close their pores to save water, which also slows their growth until conditions improve.",
    "Clouds form when warm, moist air rises and cools until the water vapour condenses into tiny droplets around specks of dust. Puffy white clouds often appear on sunny days, while thick grey layers bring steady rain. Tall towering clouds can grow into thunderstorms with lightning, heavy showers and sometimes hail. Weather forecasters study satellite images of clouds to predict how conditions will change over the next few days.",
    "Fossils are the preserved remains or traces of living things from the distant past. Bones, shells, footprints and even leaves can be turned to stone when they are buried quickly in mud or sand. By studying the layers of rock in which fossils are found, scientists can work out roughly when different creatures lived. Some fossil beds are so rich that new species are still discovered every year.",
    "The brain contains billions of nerve cells connected by trillions of tiny junctions. Signals pass along these cells as electrical pulses and cross the junctions using chemical messengers. Different areas of the brain specialise in vision, hearing, movement, language and memory, yet they work together constantly. Sleep appears to be important for storing memories and clearing away waste products that build up during the day.",
    "Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen and water over time. The reddish flakes are weaker than the original metal and can eventually eat right through it. Painting, oiling or coating iron with a thin layer of another metal slows the process. Bridges and ships therefore need regular maintenance, and engineers choose materials carefully for structures that will stand near the salty air of the sea.",
    "Seeds carry everything a young plant needs to begin life: a tiny embryo, a store of food and a protective coat. Some seeds are carried by the wind on feathery parachutes, others hitch rides on animal fur or pass through the guts of birds. Certain seeds can remain dormant in the soil for decades, waiting for the right combination of warmth, moisture and light before they germinate.",
    # daily life and home
    "On weekday mornings the kitchen becomes the busiest room in the house. Someone is always looking for a missing shoe, the kettle whistles, toast pops up and the radio plays the news. The youngest child eats cereal slowly while the older ones pack their bags for school. By half past eight the house falls silent, leaving only the cat asleep on the warm windowsill.",
    "Moving to a new flat involves more boxes than anyone expects. Books, plates, cables, clothes and forgotten souvenirs all need to be wrapped and labelled. Friends usually help carry furniture up the stairs in exchange for pizza. For the first few nights everything feels unfamiliar, from the sound of the neighbours to the position of the light switches, until slowly the new place begins to feel like home.",
    "The small garden behind the house produces more vegetables each summer than the family can eat. Tomatoes ripen against the warm brick wall, beans climb tall poles and courgettes seem to double in size overnight. Neighbours receive bags of surplus produce, and jars of chutney fill the shelves by September. Weeding is a constant task, but most evenings end with a pleasant hour of watering in the cool air.",
    "Laundry day follows a familiar rhythm in many households. Clothes are sorted by colour, socks mysteriously lose their partners and someone always leaves a tissue in a pocket. On sunny days the washing dries quickly on the line, smelling of fresh air, while in winter it hangs on racks around the house for days. Folding and putting everything away is the part that nobody volunteers for.",
    "The local library offers much more than books. Children gather for story time on Tuesday mornings, students use the quiet study rooms and older residents come in to read the newspapers. There are computers for anyone who needs them, language courses in the evenings and a small cafe near the entrance. The librarians know many regular visitors by name and often recommend novels they think they will enjoy.",
    "Taking the bus to work gives people a few minutes to read, listen to music or simply look out of the window. Regular passengers recognise each other, nodding hello without ever speaking. The driver greets familiar faces and waits an extra moment for anyone running toward the stop. On rainy days the windows steam up, and the bus fills with the smell of wet umbrellas and coats.",
    "Many families have a tradition of eating a long meal together on Sunday afternoons. Grandparents, cousins and friends gather around a table that has been extended with extra leaves. Dishes are passed from hand to hand, stories are repeated for the hundredth time and children slip away to play in the garden. The washing up afterwards becomes a social event of its own, with everyone crowded into the kitchen.",
    "Keeping houseplants alive requires a little attention but rewards it generously. Some plants like bright windows, while others prefer shady corners and only occasional watering. Overwatering is a common problem, as roots need air as well as moisture. Many people find that caring for plants is relaxing, and a room filled with green leaves feels calmer and more welcoming than one without them.",
    "The apartment building has a small courtyard where residents meet by chance throughout the day. In the morning parents gather with prams, at lunchtime office workers eat sandwiches on the bench and in the evening teenagers sit on the steps talking. A tall chestnut tree shades the space in summer, and in autumn its glossy nuts fall onto the paving stones and are collected eagerly by children.",
    "Cleaning out the attic often turns into an afternoon of unexpected memories. Old photographs, school reports, holiday postcards and toys appear from dusty boxes. Each object seems to demand a decision about whether to keep it or let it go. Most people end up keeping far more than they intended, carefully repacking the boxes and promising themselves that they will sort them properly another time.",
    "A good pair of walking shoes can make a long day in the city much more enjoyable. Tourists who explore on foot discover hidden courtyards, small bakeries and quiet churches that they would never see from a bus. By evening their legs may ache, but they have a much better sense of how the neighbourhoods fit together and where the locals actually spend their time.",
    "The corner shop stays open late every night of the week. It sells milk, bread, newspapers, batteries, birthday cards and a surprising range of other items packed onto narrow shelves. The owner has run it for twenty years and knows which customers prefer which brand of tea. During a snowstorm last winter it was the only shop in the area that opened, and people still talk about it.",
    "Learning to ride a bicycle is a milestone that many people remember clearly. A parent runs alongside holding the saddle, then lets go without saying anything, and the child pedals on alone for the first time. There are wobbles, scraped knees and the occasional fall into a hedge. Once the skill is learned, however, it seems never to be forgotten, even after many years without riding.",
    "Birthday parties for young children follow a predictable pattern of excitement and chaos. Balloons are tied to the gate, games are played in the garden and a cake with candles is carried out while everyone sings. Wrapping paper soon covers the floor, someone always cries and someone always eats too much. By the end the parents are exhausted, but the photographs are treasured for years.",
    "Working from home has changed the daily routine of many people. The commute disappears, but so does the clear separation between office and living space. Some set up a desk in a spare room, while others work at the kitchen table among breakfast dishes. Regular breaks, a short walk at lunchtime and a fixed finishing time help many of them keep a healthy balance.",
    # food and cooking
    "Bread has been baked for thousands of years using only flour, water, salt and yeast. The dough is kneaded until it becomes smooth and stretchy, then left to rise in a warm place. During baking, the crust turns brown and crisp while the inside stays soft. Each region has its own favourite shapes and grains, from dense dark rye loaves to light white rolls with a golden top.",
    "A good soup can be made from almost anything left in the kitchen. Onions, carrots and celery are softened in a little oil, then simmered with stock, beans or lentils and whatever vegetables need using up. Herbs and a squeeze of lemon brighten the flavour at the end. Served with fresh bread, a bowl of soup makes a warming meal on a cold and windy evening.",
    "Coffee beans are the seeds of a fruit that grows on shrubs in tropical highlands. After picking, the fruit is removed and the beans are dried, then roasted to develop their aroma and colour. Light roasts taste fruity and bright, while darker roasts are bitter and smoky. Around the world, people prepare coffee in countless ways, from tiny strong cups to large milky mugs.",
    "Making pasta by hand requires only flour and eggs, but it takes practice to get the texture right. The dough is rolled out into thin sheets and cut into ribbons, or shaped into small parcels filled with cheese and spinach. Fresh pasta cooks in just a couple of minutes. Many families in the countryside still gather on holidays to prepare large batches together while chatting around the table.",
    "Spices once travelled thousands of kilometres from the places where they grew to the kitchens of distant cities. Pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg were so valuable that they were sometimes used as money. Today they are inexpensive and found in every supermarket, yet they still transform simple dishes. A pinch of the right spice can make plain rice or vegetables taste completely different.",
    "The bakery on the high street opens at six in the morning, but the bakers start work in the middle of the night. By dawn the shelves are filled with croissants, rolls, loaves and cakes, and the smell of warm butter drifts down the street. Regular customers arrive on their way to work, and by noon the most popular pastries have usually sold out.",
    "Picking apples in the autumn is a favourite outing for many families. Orchards open their gates on weekends, and visitors wander between the rows with baskets, reaching for the reddest fruit. Some apples are best eaten fresh and crisp, others are better for baking pies or making sauce. Back home, the kitchen fills with the smell of cinnamon as the first apple crumble of the season comes out of the oven.",
    "Cheese is made by curdling milk, separating the curds from the whey and then pressing, salting and ageing the curds. The type of milk, the bacteria used and the length of ageing all affect the final flavour. Soft fresh cheeses are mild and creamy, while hard cheeses aged for years become sharp and crumbly. Some villages have made the same cheese in the same way for centuries.",
    "Street food markets bring together cooks from many different traditions in one lively place. Visitors can try dumplings, grilled skewers, flatbreads, curries and sweet pancakes within a few steps of each other. Long communal tables encourage strangers to sit together and compare their choices. The atmosphere is noisy and cheerful, especially on warm evenings when music plays and the queues stretch past the lantern-lit stalls.",
    "Tea is the most widely drunk beverage in the world after water. Leaves from the same plant can become green, black or oolong tea depending on how they are processed after picking. In some countries tea is taken with milk and sugar, in others it is served plain in small cups or flavoured with mint. Tea ceremonies in several cultures turn its preparation into a quiet ritual.",
    "Fresh vegetables taste best when they are cooked simply. Green beans need only a few minutes in boiling water, carrots can be roasted with a little honey and peas are delicious eaten raw straight from the pod. Overcooking turns many vegetables soft and grey, losing both flavour and colour. A sprinkle of salt and a little butter or olive oil is often all they need.",
    "Chocolate begins as bitter beans inside large pods that grow directly on the trunks of cacao trees. The beans are fermented, dried, roasted and ground into a paste, which is then mixed with sugar and sometimes milk. For centuries chocolate was drunk as a spicy beverage before solid bars became popular. Today small producers experiment with beans from different regions, much as winemakers work with grapes.",
    # sports and games
    "Football is played in almost every country, from dusty village fields to enormous stadiums packed with fans. Two teams of eleven players try to move the ball into the opposing goal using any part of the body except the hands and arms. The simplicity of the game is part of its appeal, since all that is really needed is a ball and a bit of open space.",
    "Long distance runners train for months before a marathon, gradually increasing the length of their weekly runs. On race day thousands of people gather at the start line, some aiming for a fast time and others simply hoping to finish. Spectators line the route, cheering and handing out water and slices of orange. Crossing the finish line after more than forty kilometres is an emotional moment for many.",
    "Chess has been played for well over a thousand years and is still taught in schools and clubs around the world. Each player commands an army of sixteen pieces, and the aim is to trap the opposing king. Beginners learn quickly how the pieces move, but mastering the game can take a lifetime. Tournaments sometimes last for many hours, with players sitting in complete silence.",
    "Swimming is one of the best forms of exercise because it works almost every muscle while placing little strain on the joints. Public pools are busy early in the morning with swimmers completing lengths before work. Children take lessons to learn to float, kick and breathe properly. In summer many people prefer to swim outdoors in lakes or the sea, enjoying the cooler water and open sky.",
    "Cycling races through the mountains are among the most demanding events in sport. Riders climb steep winding roads for hours, then descend at terrifying speeds on the other side. Teams work together, with some riders sheltering their leaders from the wind and fetching water bottles from the support cars. Crowds camp beside the road for days to catch a glimpse of the riders passing by.",
    "Tennis matches can last for several hours, with players running back and forth along the baseline and sprinting forward to reach short shots. The scoring system, with its strange numbers, often puzzles newcomers. Grass, clay and hard courts each favour different styles of play. At major tournaments the crowd falls silent before every serve and erupts in applause after a long and exciting rally.",
    "Board games have enjoyed a revival in recent years, with cafes dedicated to them opening in many cities. Groups of friends spend evenings building railways, trading resources or solving mysteries together around a table. Some games last twenty minutes, others an entire afternoon. Unlike screen games, they encourage people to talk, laugh and argue face to face, which many players say is the real attraction.",
    "Skiing became popular as a leisure activity in the early twentieth century, when railways made mountain villages easier to reach. Today resorts offer slopes for every level, from gentle runs for beginners to steep icy routes for experts. Ski schools are full of small children in bright helmets following their instructor in a line. At the end of the day, skiers relax by the fire with hot drinks.",
    "Basketball was invented as an indoor game to keep students active during the cold winter months. The first baskets were actual fruit baskets nailed to a balcony, and someone had to climb up to retrieve the ball after each score. Since then the game has spread worldwide and become fast and athletic, with players leaping high above the hoop to dunk the ball.",
    "Fishing from a small boat at dawn is a peaceful way to spend a morning. The water is calm, mist hangs over the surface and the only sounds are birds and the gentle slap of waves against the hull. Patience is essential, since hours can pass without a single bite. Many anglers say that catching fish is only part of the pleasure of being out on the water.",
    # arts, music and literature
    "The orchestra tunes up before the concert with a long note from the oboe, and soon every instrument joins in. When the conductor walks onto the stage the audience applauds and then falls silent. For the next hour the musicians follow the music and the movements of the baton, building from quiet passages to thunderous climaxes. Many listeners close their eyes to concentrate on the sound.",
    "Painters of the impressionist movement worked outdoors to capture the changing effects of light and weather. They used quick visible brushstrokes and bright colours, often painting the same scene at different times of day. Critics at first mocked their unfinished-looking pictures, but within a few decades the style became widely admired. Today their paintings of gardens, rivers and city streets attract huge crowds to museums.",
    "A good novel can transport a reader to another country, another century or an entirely imaginary world. Readers come to care about the characters, worrying about their troubles and celebrating their small victories. Some books are devoured in a single sitting, while others are read slowly over months. Book clubs meet in living rooms and libraries to discuss favourite chapters and argue about the ending.",
    "Learning to play the piano takes years of patient practice. Beginners start with simple tunes using one hand at a time, gradually learning to read music and coordinate both hands. Scales and exercises may feel tedious, but they build the strength and agility needed for harder pieces. Many adults who stopped lessons as children return to the instrument later in life and find great enjoyment in it.",
    "The theatre company performs in a converted warehouse near the docks. The stage is small and the audience sits close enough to see every expression on the actors' faces. Costumes are made by volunteers, and the set is built from recycled wood and fabric. Despite the modest budget, the productions are lively and inventive, and tickets for the opening nights sell out within hours.",
    "Pottery is one of the oldest crafts in the world. Clay is shaped by hand or on a spinning wheel, left to dry and then fired in a kiln at high temperature to become hard. Glazes add colour and make the surface waterproof. Every region has its own traditional patterns and shapes, and archaeologists often use broken pieces of pottery to date the places where they are found.",
    "Jazz grew out of a mixture of musical traditions in port cities at the start of the twentieth century. Musicians blended rhythms, melodies and instruments in new ways, and improvisation became central to the style. Small bands played in crowded clubs late into the night, each player taking turns to create a solo on the spot. Recordings soon spread the sound across the world.",
    "Photography changed the way people remembered their lives. Early cameras were large and slow, and subjects had to sit perfectly still for long exposures, which is why old portraits rarely show smiles. Later cameras became small enough to carry everywhere, and families filled albums with holidays, weddings and birthdays. Today almost everyone carries a camera in their pocket and takes pictures every day.",
    "Folk songs were passed from generation to generation by singing rather than writing. They told stories of love, work, the sea, distant wars and local heroes, and their words often changed slightly from village to village. Collectors in the nineteenth century travelled the countryside writing them down before they were forgotten. Many of those tunes are still sung today at festivals and around campfires.",
    "Poetry uses rhythm, sound and imagery to say a great deal in few words. Some poems rhyme and follow strict patterns, while others flow freely like ordinary speech. A single line can capture the feeling of a rainy afternoon or the memory of a childhood garden. Many people learn short poems by heart at school and find that the words stay with them for decades.",
    "Dance appears in every human culture, from formal ballroom steps to energetic street styles. In some traditions dances tell stories of harvests, battles or legends, while in others they are simply a way to celebrate. Ballet dancers train for many years to achieve strength and grace, and their performances look effortless only because of the enormous amount of practice behind them.",
    "Comic strips began as short humorous panels in newspapers and grew into a rich art form. Artists combine drawings and words to tell stories that can be funny, adventurous or deeply moving. Some characters have appeared in print for generations and become familiar to millions of readers. Collectors hunt for rare early issues, while new artists publish their work online for audiences around the world.",
    # technology and transport
    "Smartphones combine a telephone, camera, map, music player and computer in a device small enough to fit in a pocket. They have changed how people communicate, shop, navigate and spend their free time. Some worry that constant notifications make it harder to concentrate, and many families now set rules about when phones can be used at the dinner table or in the bedroom.",
    "Electric cars are becoming more common on the roads of many cities. They are quiet, produce no exhaust fumes and can be charged at home overnight. Drivers who switch from petrol cars often mention how smoothly they accelerate. The main challenges are the cost of batteries and the time needed to recharge on long journeys, although charging stations are steadily appearing along major highways.",
    "The bridge spans more than a kilometre across the wide estuary, hanging from two enormous towers by thick steel cables. Before it was built, travellers had to wait for a ferry or drive a long way around to the nearest crossing. Engineers spent years studying the wind, the tides and the soft riverbed before construction began. On foggy mornings the towers disappear into the clouds.",
    "Airports are small cities in their own right, with shops, restaurants, hotels and thousands of workers. Every day planes arrive and depart according to carefully planned schedules, guided by controllers who watch the sky from a tall tower. Baggage travels through hidden networks of conveyor belts beneath the terminals. For passengers, the waiting can feel endless, but the journey itself may cross half the world in a day.",
    "Satellites circle the earth carrying instruments that observe weather, relay telephone calls and broadcast television. Some orbit low and fast, completing a circuit in about ninety minutes, while others remain fixed above one point on the equator. Navigation systems in cars and phones work by receiving signals from several satellites at once. On clear nights some can be seen as moving points of light.",
    "Wind turbines turn the movement of air into electricity. Their long blades rotate slowly, driving a generator inside the housing at the top of a tall tower. Wind farms are built on hills, plains and increasingly out at sea, where the wind blows more steadily. Together with solar panels, they supply a growing share of the electricity used in homes, offices and factories.",
    "The underground railway carries millions of passengers beneath the city every day. Trains arrive every few minutes during the rush hour, and the platforms fill and empty in a constant rhythm. Maps of the network use simple coloured lines that ignore real distances, which makes them easy to read. Musicians play in the long tunnels between lines, and their music echoes off the tiled walls.",
    "Printing in three dimensions allows objects to be built layer by layer from plastic, metal or even food. Designers create a model on a computer, and the printer follows it precisely, adding thin layers one at a time. Engineers use the technique to make prototypes quickly, doctors to produce custom implants and hobbyists to create toys, tools and replacement parts for household gadgets.",
    "Container ships carry most of the goods traded between countries. Each ship can hold thousands of standard steel boxes stacked high on deck and deep in the hold. Giant cranes at the ports lift the containers on and off in a carefully planned order. The boxes then continue their journeys by train or truck, so that a shirt or a television may travel halfway around the world in weeks.",
    "The first computers filled entire rooms and were operated by teams of specialists. They used thousands of glowing valves that frequently burned out and had to be replaced. Over the following decades components shrank dramatically, and computers moved from laboratories into offices and then into homes. Today a small watch contains more computing power than the machines that once guided spacecraft to the moon.",
    "Canals were dug across many countries before railways existed, allowing heavy goods to be moved by horse-drawn boats. Locks raise and lower boats between different water levels, using gates and the force of gravity. Many old canals have been restored for leisure, and people now hire narrow boats for slow holidays, cruising through the countryside at walking pace and mooring beside village pubs.",
    "Hot air balloons rise because the heated air inside the envelope is lighter than the cooler air around it. Pilots control the height by firing a burner or letting air cool, but they cannot steer and must follow the wind. Early morning flights are popular, when the air is calm. Passengers stand in a wicker basket and watch the fields and villages pass silently below.",
    # animals
    "Elephants live in close family groups led by the oldest female. They communicate with deep rumbling sounds that can travel several kilometres through the ground. Young elephants are looked after not only by their mothers but also by aunts and older sisters. Elephants need large amounts of food and water every day, and they remember the locations of waterholes for many years.",
    "Honeybees live in colonies that may contain tens of thousands of individuals. Worker bees collect nectar and pollen from flowers, build wax combs and care for the young. A single queen lays all the eggs. Bees tell each other where to find good flowers by performing a special dance inside the hive, describing both the direction and the distance to the food source.",
    "Owls hunt mainly at night, using their excellent hearing and large eyes to find mice and other small animals. Their soft feathers allow them to fly almost silently. Many owls can turn their heads very far around, since their eyes cannot move within their sockets. In folklore owls are often linked with wisdom, though in some cultures they are considered a sign of bad luck.",
    "Dolphins are highly social animals that live and hunt in groups called pods. They use a wide range of whistles and clicks to communicate and to find fish by echo. Young dolphins stay with their mothers for several years, learning how to catch food and navigate. Sailors have long told stories of dolphins swimming alongside ships and playing in the waves made by the bows.",
    "Penguins cannot fly, but they are superb swimmers, using their wings as flippers to chase fish underwater. Many species live in cold southern seas and breed in large colonies on ice or rocky shores. Parents take turns keeping the egg warm and fetching food from the ocean. Their dense feathers and a layer of fat protect them from the freezing wind and water.",
    "Foxes have adapted remarkably well to life in towns and cities. They raise their cubs in gardens, under sheds and along railway embankments, and they feed on scraps, insects and small animals. Most city dwellers rarely see them, since they are most active at night. Their sharp barks and screams in winter can be startling to anyone who has never heard them before.",
    "Migrating birds travel enormous distances each year between their breeding grounds and their winter homes. Some small songbirds cross deserts and seas without stopping, while geese fly in long V-shaped formations that save energy. Scientists use tiny rings and electronic tags to follow their routes. The return of familiar birds each spring is welcomed by many people as a sign that warmer weather is coming.",
    "Horses have worked alongside people for thousands of years, pulling ploughs and carts and carrying riders across great distances. Different breeds were developed for speed, strength or endurance. Today most horses are kept for sport and leisure, and riding schools teach children how to groom, saddle and care for them. A healthy horse can live for more than twenty-five years.",
    "Octopuses are among the most unusual creatures in the sea. They have eight flexible arms lined with suckers, can change the colour and texture of their skin in an instant and squeeze through tiny gaps. In aquariums they have been seen opening jars and exploring their tanks with apparent curiosity. Most species live only a year or two, spending much of that time alone.",
    # travel and cities
    "The old harbour town is a maze of narrow lanes, steep stairways and colourful houses stacked up the hillside. Cafes spill onto the waterfront, where fishing boats unload their catch in the early morning. In the evening locals and visitors stroll along the promenade, eating ice cream and watching the lights of the ships far out at sea. Every summer a festival fills the streets with music.",
    "Night trains offer a relaxing way to travel long distances. Passengers board in the evening, eat dinner in the restaurant car and then settle into narrow bunks while the train rolls through the dark countryside. Waking up in a different city, with a cup of coffee and new scenery outside the window, feels like a small adventure. Some routes have recently been revived after years of absence.",
    "Large cities never truly sleep. Bakeries prepare bread before dawn, cleaners sweep the empty streets and delivery trucks restock shops long before they open. During the day the pavements are crowded with commuters, tourists and street vendors. In the evening theatres, restaurants and bars light up, and the noise of traffic continues until late. Even at three in the morning a few windows remain bright.",
    "Visiting a museum can be overwhelming because there is so much to see. Many experienced visitors choose just one or two rooms and spend time looking closely at a few objects rather than rushing past everything. Guided tours, audio guides and small labels help explain the history behind each piece. Afterwards the museum cafe offers a welcome chance to sit down and talk about what impressed them most.",
    "Camping in the countryside brings people closer to nature for a few days. Tents are pitched beside a stream or in a quiet meadow, and meals are cooked over a small stove or campfire. At night the sky is full of stars far brighter than in the city. Mornings start early with birdsong, and even simple tasks like fetching water become part of the adventure.",
    "The mountain village is reached by a winding road with dozens of hairpin bends. Its wooden houses have wide balconies decorated with red flowers, and cows graze in the meadows just beyond the last barn. In winter the village becomes a quiet ski resort, while in summer hikers set off from the square to explore the surrounding peaks, lakes and glaciers before returning for a hearty dinner.",
]

